/-
# Simple groups generated by two elements of prime-power order (Kourovka Notebook Problem 18.52)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 18.52 (J. Krempa): is every finite simple
group generated by two elements of prime-power orders `m`, `n`? (Here `m`, `n` may depend on the
group.)
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.OrderOfElement
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Subgroup.Simple

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.SimpleGroupPrimePowerGeneratorsKourovka18Problem52Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 18.52 (J.
Krempa). UnsolvedMath record UM-00701481 (`KOU-18.52`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Finite simple groups are `IsSimpleGroup` (the cyclic groups of prime order are included, and are
generated by one element of prime order, taken twice); prime powers are `IsPrimePow`, so exponent at
least `1`.
-/

/-- **Kourovka Problem 18.52 (generation by two elements of prime-power order).** The statement
that every finite simple group $G$ is generated by two elements $x, y \in G$ whose orders are prime
powers, that is, each of the form $p^k$ with $p$ prime and $k \ge 1$ (the primes and exponents may
differ between $x$ and $y$ and depend on the group). The problem is due to J. Krempa. -/
def simpleGroupGeneratedByPrimePowerOrderElements : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G], IsSimpleGroup G →
    ∃ x y : G, IsPrimePow (orderOf x) ∧ IsPrimePow (orderOf y) ∧ Subgroup.closure {x, y} = ⊤

/--
**Finite simple groups are generated by two elements of prime-power order.** Every finite simple
group $G$ is generated by two elements $x, y \in G$ whose orders are prime powers, that is, each
of the form $p^k$ with $p$ prime and $k \ge 1$, where the prime and the exponent may differ
between $x$ and $y$. This answers Kourovka Problem 18.52 positively.

The Kourovka Archive answers 18.52 "Yes, it is" (mod CFSG): by C. S. H. King (2017) every finite
(non-abelian) simple group is generated by an involution and an element of prime order; a cyclic
group of prime order is generated by one element of prime order.

Source: E. I. Khukhro and
V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue,
arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 18.52 (J. Krempa); resolution: C. S.
H. King, J. Algebra 478 (2017), 153-173, Kourovka Archive, Problem 18.52: Yes, it is (mod CFSG);
moreover every finite simple group is generated by an involution and an element of prime order,
https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v49.
-/
public theorem_wanted simpleGroupGeneratedByPrimePowerOrderElements_holds :
    simpleGroupGeneratedByPrimePowerOrderElements

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.SimpleGroupPrimePowerGeneratorsKourovka18Problem52Wanted
